<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors in My Classroom – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Participate in choral reading of familiar short stories with support and guidance from the teacher. Phonemic Awareness: Identify environmental sounds and speech sounds.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify pictures of classroom objects in a story when read aloud (e.g., hear the word "pencil" in the story and can point to "pencil"). Can point to characters and objects during choral reading (e.g., pointing to a red backpack when the word "backpack" is read; pointing to a green crayon during a story about classroom materials). Can match familiar environmental sounds and speech sounds to pictures with support (e.g., pointing to a picture of a bell after hearing the bell ring, or matching the /b/ sound to a picture of a "book").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Find the Color" game. The teacher says, "Point to something red!" and students point to red objects in the classroom (e.g., a red crayon, a red bag). The teacher calls out "blue," "yellow" for other colors and "pencil," "book," "chair," "desk" for objects, encouraging students to point. This activates previously learned vocabulary and TPR skills. The teacher models saying "thank you" when a student points correctly, e.g., "Thank you for pointing to the red pencil!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of a "pencil" and a "book," clearly articulating the initial sounds /p/ and /b/. The teacher then models pointing to corresponding objects in the classroom while saying their names. For the upcoming story, the teacher models how to point to pictures in a book as the words are read, demonstrating tracking from left to right with a finger.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a red crayon) "Is this a blue crayon or a red crayon?" (Students point to red or say "red.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a book) "Do we look at the pictures or do we point to the pictures?" (Students point to the pictures.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in My Classroom."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher presents a large illustrated storybook titled "My Colorful Classroom!" featuring simple, repetitive sentences that incorporate classroom objects (pencil, book, chair, desk, crayon, board, table, bag) and colors (red, blue, yellow), as well as prepositions (on, under, in, next to). For example: "Look! A red pencil is on the table." "Oh, a blue book is under the chair." The story will also have pictures that can be associated with environmental sounds like a school bell or chairs moving.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large illustrated storybook ("Big Book") with clear pictures of classroom objects in different colors and simple sentences. Flashcards for initial sounds /p/, /b/, /t/, /f/ (e.g., picture of a **p**encil, **b**ook, **t**able, **f**ish/fan for /f/) and environmental sounds (school bell, chair moving).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying classroom objects and colors; following directions (e.g., "Point to the red pencil"); matching environmental sounds and speech sounds to pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (during reading):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads a page, then pauses: "What color is the book?" (Students point to the color in the picture and/or respond with "red" or "blue.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "The pencil is on the desk." "Where is the pencil?" (Students point to the pencil on the desk in the illustration.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of a chair: "Is this a chair? Yes or No?" (Students respond chorally or with gestures.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading familiar words (nouns, colors) through picture identification, understanding prepositions, initial phonemic awareness (/p/, /b/, /t/, /f/), identifying environmental sounds, and responding to "Yes/No" and "What color is it?" questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Reading & Pointing:</b> The teacher rereads "My Colorful Classroom!" story. This time, students are encouraged to point to each picture as the teacher reads the corresponding word. The teacher reads slowly and clearly, emphasizing target vocabulary: "This is a <b style="color: red;">red</b> <b style="color: #1a5276;">pencil</b>." Students point to the red pencil in the book. "The <b style="color: blue;">blue</b> <b style="color: #1a5276;">book</b> is <b style="color: #1a5276;">on</b> the <b style="color: #1a5276;">table</b>." Students point to the blue book on the table. Teacher asks: "What color is this (crayon)?" and students respond chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sound Match Activity:</b> The teacher shows flashcards with pictures representing environmental sounds (e.g., school bell, chairs moving) and initial speech sounds (e.g., a **p**encil for /p/, a **b**ook for /b/, a **t**able for /t/, a **f**ish for /f/). The teacher plays the actual environmental sounds (bell ringing, chairs scraping) or clearly articulates the initial sounds. Students listen and point to the matching flashcard. For example, teacher says "/p/, /p/, /p/," and students point to the "pencil" flashcard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Is This Your...?" Picture Game:</b> Students receive a worksheet with various classroom objects (pencil, book, crayon, bag) in different colors. The teacher holds up a flashcard, e.g., a "yellow crayon." The teacher asks, "Is this your yellow crayon?" Students point to the yellow crayon on their worksheet. If the teacher holds a "red book" and asks, "Is this your blue book?", students shake their heads or point to the red book and say "No." This reinforces Yes/No questions and object/color identification.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in My Classroom."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Classroom Color Detective"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in small groups (with teacher assistance) to become "Classroom Color Detectives." The teacher distributes large picture cards of classroom objects (e.g., a red crayon, a blue book, a yellow chair) and a simple list of directions using prepositions (e.g., "Find a red crayon," "Look for the blue book under the desk"). Students will "read" the picture cards and follow the verbal instructions by physically moving around the classroom to find the actual objects. They will point to the objects and confirm the color. Teacher facilitates by asking: "What is this?" and "What color is it?" for each found item.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided physical search and identification activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> Directly applies the vocabulary of classroom objects and colors to the real classroom environment, reinforcing prepositional understanding and object identification through active participation and physical "reading" of the environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> Students will demonstrate understanding by accurately pointing to and identifying objects/colors when prompted. The teacher encourages choral responses as groups find items. Teacher models "Thank you for finding the blue book!" after each successful find.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Color Detective" activity and a brief individual "Show Me" task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives individual students a picture sheet with various classroom objects (pencil, book, chair, crayon). The teacher will give verbal instructions like: "Point to the red pencil." "Show me the blue book." "Where is the crayon?" The teacher will also make an initial sound like /b/ and ask, "Point to the picture that starts with /b/."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in pointing to specified objects/colors (e.g., red pencil).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify pictures associated with initial sounds (/p/, /b/, /t/, /f/) and environmental sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in choral responses and following multi-step directions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper with a picture of a storybook and a crayon. They circle a happy face if they liked our story about colors and classroom objects, or a sad face if they found it difficult. The teacher asks, "What was your favorite color we read about today?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite color to point to in our story today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a blue pencil in the classroom right now?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The choral reading and pointing activities worked well for most students, especially with the large illustrations. The "Classroom Color Detective" was highly engaging and reinforced the prepositions. Some students struggled with differentiating initial sounds /t/ and /f/; I will need to provide more explicit modeling and practice for these specific sounds in future lessons. I noticed that including "thank you" as a prompt helped reinforce politeness. For the next lesson, I will ensure more interactive speaking opportunities are prepared to transition from reading comprehension to active verbal production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, we will use our knowledge of classroom objects and colors to practice speaking and interacting with our friends, asking and answering questions like "What color is your book?" and "Is this your pencil?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[2-3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>